{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs. outcomes, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger from days 10–30).",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when the recent 30d history is in a clear decline, the forecast path declines too slowly and stays elevated. Increase the negative slope (steepen the projected drawdown) in trending-down regimes.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model tends to project stronger/longer rebounds (local peaks around days ~20–30) than realized. Damp positive day-to-day changes after a short-term uptick; cap rebound amplitude and shorten rebound duration.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: forecasts revert upward toward a higher implied mean than the market actually follows in these samples. Lower the long-run anchor/mean used for reversion during weak-price regimes.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: errors are modest early (days 1–5) but grow materially later (days 10–30). Use a horizon-weighted bias correction (increasing penalty with lead time) rather than a single constant adjustment."
  ]
}
